ryla rodinger and his supporters went to  kenya's supreme court just before the  deadline for challenging the election  results  he submitted dozens of boxes of  documents as evidence along with his  petition it asks the court to nullify  the election's outcome on several  grounds there are forgery of election  documents  there was  interference with the integrity of the  technology  that was used in the elections  there was also a  falsification of results  which were uploaded into  the networks that were operated by the  electoral commission  kenyans voted in elections two weeks ago  ryla rodinga has run for president four  times before leading the opposition  this time he had the backing of his  historical rival outgoing president  uhuru  forward his kenyatta opponent was deputy  president william ruto  last week the electoral commission  declared him the winner with just over  50 percent of the vote  [Music]  ryla's supporters don't believe it  many of them believe he's been rigged  out of election victories before  he's challenged the results of the last  three elections in court supporters of  riley redinger hope that the court  process will favor them as it did five  years ago and rider rolling a challenge  the election results and the supreme  court ruled that there have been serious  irregularities and it ordered a re-run  that time he boycotted the rerun saying  the electoral commission failed to fix  the problems  this time he said he has the evidence to  prove criminality in the electoral  process the corruption cartels are  prepared to compromise electoral systems  bribe election officials  make security systems look other way  or even kill in order to  find their way to power  ryla's petition is one of several  submitted to the court respondents  including the electoral commission and  william ruto have four days to submit  their responses the court has two weeks  to complete its hearing and give a  verdict it could uphold the election  results or order a recount of votes  or a rerun of the polls  malcolm webb al jazeera nairobi kenya
 

